  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA low-level shortwave is moving around and upper level trough \nthat is situated across Eastern Canada. This shortwave will \nsupport a surface trough that will cross Lake Erie early this \nmorning and move eastward rather quickly. Along with this \ntrough, a pool of -8 to - 10C 850mb temperatures will move \nsouthward with it creating enough instability over the lake that\nhas temperatures in the low 50s to generate lake effect showers\ndownstream. These showers are currently impacting Northeast \nOhio and Northwestern Pennsylvania and will wane into the \nmorning hours as the support and moisture moves off. The showers\nare starting off as rain but will switch over to a rain/snow \nmix or wet snow in the higher elevations of Pennsylvania and \nOhio the few hours before sunrise. Minimal accumulation is \nexpected, generally around a half an inch at the most. \n\nHigh pressure will begin to build in from the west today that will \ncut off the flow and moisture from Lake Erie and effectively end the \nlake effect showers. Dry weather is expected for the remainder of \nthe day and through Wednesday evening. Temperatures will remain \nchilly, with highs in the mid to low 40s. Tuesday night should see \ndecent radiational cooling with the high pressure over head and \nlow temperatures will drop down into the mid to low 20s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWednesday evening, a strong, closed upper level trough will deepen \nacross the mid-Mississippi Valley as a surface low moves north along \nthe east coast. This surface low will track northeasterly through \nNew England by Saturday. A secondary surface low pressure system \nwill develop across the Ohio Valley and push into the Eastern Great \nLakes. A strong low level jet wrapping around the low will bring \nstrong moisture advection into the region Thursday. This will bring \nwidespread rain showers across the region beginning early Thursday \nmorning and persist through overnight. \n\nWrap around moisture is expected as the low deepens to the northeast \nallowing for showers to continue into Friday. Flow will be out of \nthe west to northwest at this time so mainly expecting precipitation \nto fall as rain with some rain/snow mix possible early Friday \nmorning. This will also allow for some lake enhancement as 850mb \ntemperatures drop down to  -2 to -4C. Flow shifts around to be out \nof the northwest throughout the day Friday with precipitation \nswitching over to being lake effect driven. Guidance still suggests \nthat precipitation will mainly be rain, though some wet snow will be \npossible in the higher elevations of Northeastern Ohio and \nNorthwestern Pennsylvania. Not expecting much accumulation with the \nsnow as the surface temperatures will be at freezing or just above \nSaturday morning. \n\nCurrent trends have lake effect showers continue as a trough will \npersist over the eastern portion of the lake through the weekend \ninto early next week.",
